---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="The kinds of art"
  description="A quick tour of the categories of 2D images in the game, with rough counts and where each lives."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: '2D Art', href: `${base}/field-guide/2d-art/` },
    { label: 'Kinds of art' },
  ]}
>
  <h2>Item icons</h2>
  <p>
    There are <strong>656 item icons</strong>, all 32×32 pixels, all packed
    into a single file: <code>item/itemicon.ofs</code>. Every shop screen,
    every inventory tile, every shopkeeper's display is drawing from this
    set.
  </p>
  <p>
    The icons themselves carry no palette. Their colors come from a
    256-color palette in <code>global.lzs</code> (entry 5, plus entry 6
    for the dimmed/unavailable variant). This took some hunting to figure
    out — see <a href={`${base}/field-guide/2d-art/palettes/`}>finding the
    right palette</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Magic glyphs (the keyboard)</h2>
  <p>
    The "magic language" keyboard the player uses to compose spells has
    <strong>377 glyph buttons</strong>, drawn from a pool of icons across
    <code>2d/inputmagic/mgcall.ofs</code> and the <code>mgc01..09c.lzs</code>
    family. All of them share a single 16-color palette in
    <code>2d/inputmagic/mgc80.ofs</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each glyph is a noun or short verb — "greeting," "magic," "yes,"
    "Vivian," "morning," "river," and so on. The
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/names-and-words/magic-glyphs/`}>magic
    glyph page</a> covers the full inventory and how we translated it.
  </p>

  <h2>NPC portraits</h2>
  <p>
    Character portraits — the tutorial face, the close-up dialogue
    portraits, the sticker / stamp variants — live in containers like
    <code>2d/pic2d.ofs</code>. They're stored as background screens
    (256×192 tilemaps), not sprites. Each portrait is a triplet of
    files within the container: a tile sheet, a palette, and a tilemap
    that lays the tiles out.
  </p>

  <h2>Background screens</h2>
  <p>
    Full 256×192 still images — title art, shop interiors, menu backdrops,
    cooking-minigame stages — are stored as a tile sheet plus a tilemap.
    The tilemap is a 32×24 grid of 16-bit cells, where each cell says
    "use this tile, with this palette bank, flipped this way."
  </p>
  <p>
    The title screen is a good example: <code>2d/mainmenu/title_pack.ofs</code>
    holds five seasonal/time-of-day variants (day castle, dawn, dusk, night
    castle, sunburst), and the game picks one based on the system clock.
  </p>

  <h2>UI sprites assembled from recipes</h2>
  <p>
    A lot of UI screens aren't single images. They're assemblies — half
    a dozen sprite chips placed at specific X/Y coordinates on a 256×192
    canvas. The shop's "buy item" screen is the worked example. Its
    composition recipe is a 10-entry table baked into one of the game's
    code overlays. Each entry says "place sprite chip N at (X, Y) using
    palette M." More on this in
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/2d-art/composition-recipes/`}>composition
    recipes</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>UI sprites assembled at runtime</h2>
  <p>
    Other UI screens — the chat window, the chest, anything that resizes
    or scrolls — don't have a static recipe. The placement comes from the
    game's code, which decides on the fly where each chip goes. To capture
    those screens exactly as they appear, we'd need to trace them in an
    emulator. We have all the parts (every individual chip extracts to a
    PNG), just not the assembly instructions yet.
  </p>

  <h2>3D models, which are out of scope here</h2>
  <p>
    A handful of things you might think are 2D are actually 3D: cooked
    dishes (<code>dishup_mdl.ofs</code>), the cooking-minigame food
    (<code>uccmdl.ofs</code>), and most of the world (the school grounds,
    the town, etc.). These are Nintendo NSBMD models with NSBTX textures
    and aren't covered in the 2D pipeline. The title splash's "EVENT HALL"
    backdrop is also 3D, even though the logo on top of it is 2D.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
